# Vision

A factory that takes in ordinary industrial feedstock and puts out sheets of
graphene, cut to whatever size is asked for. Not a product. A design: the
machines, what each one must do, and the list of things that must become true
before the material is allowed to move to the next machine.

This is a paper facility. There is no simulation. Every machine is described
by four things and only four things:

  - what enters it
  - what leaves it
  - what must be true for it to work at all
  - how much floor it stands on

## The route

Chemical vapour deposition on copper.

Methane and hydrogen are flowed over copper held near 1000 C. Copper barely
dissolves carbon at all, so the methane cracks on the copper's surface and the
loose carbon atoms have nowhere to go but sideways. They stitch themselves into
a single sheet. When the sheet has covered the copper there is no bare copper
left to crack methane on, so growth stops on its own. That is why the product
is one atom thick without anybody timing it. The self-limiting behaviour is a
gift from the phase diagram, not a control system.

This route was chosen over the three alternatives because it is the only one
that yields a continuous sheet rather than a powder, a wafer-bound film, or a
holed and half-healed one.

## The problem this factory exists to attack

The standard way to get the graphene off the copper is to destroy the copper.
Acid dissolves the foil out from under the sheet and the copper leaves as ions
in solution, never to be a foil again. The foil is about 25 micrometres thick.
The graphene is 0.335 nanometres thick. Roughly seventy-five thousand atomic
layers of copper are consumed to harvest one atomic layer of carbon.

That ratio is the whole design problem. A factory that does this is not a
graphene factory, it is a copper-destroying factory that produces graphene as a
by-product.

## The idea the factory is built around

From the user, verbatim:

  "what if the acid pulled away the copper at a certain temperature, and
  re-applied it at another? We might be able to remove the copper from the face
  of a large block, one face at a time, extract the graphene, then re-apply the
  copper to the structure, improving the efficiency of the production process."

So: the copper is not stock. The copper is apparatus. A block of it sits in the
machine and stays there. Graphene is grown on a face, that face's outermost
copper is taken away just far enough to let the sheet go, the sheet is
harvested, and the copper is put back where it came from. The block ends each
cycle the same mass and the same shape it started.

The copper is skimmed, not spent.

## What is still open

Written down here as they surface. Each one gets asked and settled before the
design can be called finished.

  - Whether the reversal is driven by temperature, by electric current, or by a
    gas that carries copper between a hot end and a cold end.
  - What holds the sheet up during the moment its copper is gone. A sheet one
    atom thick with nothing underneath it is a membrane in free space.
  - Whether re-deposited copper comes back as the crystal face that grows good
    graphene, or as a randomly oriented crust that grows bad graphene. If the
    second, the block degrades every cycle and the factory has a shelf life.
  - How many faces of the block are in production at once, and what the other
    faces are doing while one is being harvested.
  - Where the carbon comes from, and whether the hydrogen leaving the cracking
    reaction is captured or vented.

## The decision on how many designs to build

From the user:

  "we should build designs for all the available systems and then try and
  figure out how to connect them and draw lessons or improvements from each
  other."

So the factory is not one line with a chosen mechanism. It is one line with a
**slot**, and three machines designed to occupy it. Everything upstream of the
slot and everything downstream of it is shared, which is what makes the three
comparable rather than merely three separate factories.

The comparison is the deliverable, not a tiebreaker before the deliverable.
